# Nouns Reference Content

> The per-paradigm tables under "Noun Paradigms by Gender" are reference data for
> `AGREEMENT_PARADIGMS`, not the page layout: the page renders them as endings grids
> (see "Page Structure"). Where this file disagrees with `components/nouns-section.tsx`,
> the code is the truth.

Noun declensions by gender and case - how Greek nouns change their endings.

## Purpose

Show how Greek nouns decline according to gender, case, and number. The ending patterns reveal the underlying system.

## Key Insight

Greek nouns change their endings to show grammatical function (case) and number. Learn the pattern by gender and the system becomes predictable.

---

## Page Structure

<page file="tabs/nouns.tsx">
1. ReferenceHero "Endings by gender." with a `demo`: ο φίλος / τον φίλο / του φίλου, each coloured and labelled by its case row (Doer, Target, Owner), from `CASE_ROW_BY_KEY` and the masc-os paradigm.
2. `NounsSection`: SectionHeading "How Noun Endings Change" (subtitle "Patterns by gender and case").
3. `CaseGuide` (eyebrow "Concept", "Which case should I use?"): Doer / Target / Owner badges with the grammar term beneath, a question each (Who does it? / Who/what receives? / Whose is it?) and a frame around ο φίλος: ο φίλος μιλάει, βλέπω τον φίλο, το σπίτι του φίλου. Footer: all prepositions (σε, με, για, από…) take accusative.
4. `GenderHints` (eyebrow "Spotting gender", "Recognise gender by ending").
5. `EssentialPatterns` (eyebrow "The core", "Essential patterns"): masc-os, fem-a, neut-o, neut-i as columns (header shows the ending plus a corpus example word), case rows, singular grid stacked above plural. An Endings / Full forms toggle; Full forms adds a "Calling" (Vocative) row to the singular grid only. Footer: feminine and neuter Doer = Target, only Owner changes; masculine is the only family where Target differs.
6. "More patterns" (collapsible): one `GenderVariants` card per gender with every paradigm of that gender, endings only, footer listing each pattern's examples, corpus count ("N nouns in this course") and tip.
7. Handoff NextStepCards: Pronouns (emphasis) → /reference/pronouns; Articles → /reference/articles.
8. PracticeCTA "Practice nouns".

Cell emphasis has three weights: the nominative is medium, a cell whose ending differs from the nominative is semibold, a cell identical to it is muted.
</page>

